mastery.solution_text:
In $\triangle ABC$ and $\triangle ADC$:
$AB = AD$ (equal sides of a kite)
$CB = CD$ (equal sides of a kite)
$AC$ is common.
$\therefore \triangle ABC \cong \triangle ADC$ (SSS)
$\therefore \angle BAE = \angle DAE$ (matching angles of congruent triangles)
In $\triangle ABE$ and $\triangle ADE$:
$AB = AD$ (equal sides of a kite)
$\angle BAE = \angle DAE$ (proved above)
$AE$ is common.
$\therefore \triangle ABE \cong \triangle ADE$ (SAS)
$\therefore \angle AEB = \angle AED$ (matching angles of congruent triangles)
$\angle AEB + \angle AED = 180^{\circ}$ (angles on a straight line $BD$)
$\therefore \angle AEB = \angle AED = 90^{\circ}$
